text:
Police have issued a public warning after the release of a high-risk sex offender who has four convictions for preying on elderly women.
Vancouver police say James Burrows, 57, a federal offender convicted of four sexual assaults against women between the ages of 72 and 93, has been released under strict conditions and will be living in the city.
Burrows was assessed by the Correctional Service of Canada as a “high risk for violent and sexual re-offending,” said VPD spokesman Const. Brian Montague in a release Friday.
He especially “poses a risk of significant harm to the safety of elderly women,” said the warning.
Burrows is black, six-foot-one, 260 pounds, with black hair and brown eyes.
He was released under a long list of conditions that include:
• Not to associate with any person known to be involved in criminal activity;
• Not to consume or possess alcohol or illegal drugs;
• Not to enter any liquor store, beer and/or wine store, or business whose primary purpose is the sale or distribution of alcoholic beverages;
• No contact directly or indirectly with any of his victims or their families;
• Immediately report any attempts to initiate intimate sexual and non-sexual relationships with females to his parole supervisor;
• And not to purchase, acquire, possess, or access pornography or sexually explicit material in any form or type of media.
Anyone who sees Burrows violating these conditions is asked to call 911.
